  "Number": 36,
  "Question": "Determine \\(m\\) so that \\(4x^2-6x+m\\) is divisible by \\(x-3.\\) The obtained value, \\(m,\\) is an exact divisor of:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(12\\)",
    "B": "\\(20\\)",
    "C": "\\(36\\)",
    "D": "\\(48\\)",
    "E": "\\(64\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "If \\(x-3\\) is a factor, substituting \\(x=3\\) must make the polynomial zero, because the factor \\(x-3\\) becomes zero. Therefore\n\\[\n4(3)^2-6(3)+m=0,\n\\]\nso \\(18+m=0\\) and \\(m=-18\\). This value does work, since\n\\[\n4x^2-6x-18=(x-3)(4x+6).\n\\]\nA negative integer can be an exact divisor: the quotient must be an integer. Among the choices, only \\(36\\) is a multiple of \\(18\\), and\n\\[\n\\frac{36}{-18}=-2.\n\\]\nThus the answer is \\(36\\), choice C.",
